.TH FMAN 1 "2026-09-29" "fman @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fman \- terminal file manager built on a UI independent core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fman
.RI [ command " [" argument " ...]]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fman
is a small file manager for Linux.
Started without arguments it prints a prompt and executes commands until you
leave it, so the current directory is kept between commands.
Started with a command it executes that one command, prints the result and
exits with the status of that command, which makes it usable from scripts.
.PP
Paths may be absolute, relative, or start with
.BR ~ " for the home directory."
The special path
.B -
refers to the previous directory.
Arguments may be quoted with single or double quotes and backslash escapes
are honoured outside single quotes.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI ls " [\-a] [\-l] [path]"
List the entries of a directory.
Directories are marked with a trailing
.BR / ,
symbolic links with a trailing
.BR @ .
.TP
.B ll
Shorthand for
.BR "ls \-l" .
.TP
.BI cd " [path]"
Change the current directory.
Without an argument it goes to the home directory.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the current directory.
.TP
.BI cp " [\-r] [\-f] [\-p] " source " ... " destination
Copy files and directories.
When several sources or an existing directory as destination are given the
sources are copied into the destination, like
.BR cp (1).
Symbolic links are recreated rather than followed.
.TP
.BI mv " [\-f] " source " ... " destination
Move or rename files and directories.
Within one file system this uses
.BR rename (2);
across file systems it falls back to copy and delete.
.TP
.BI rm " [\-r] [\-f] " path " ..."
Remove files, or whole trees with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI mkdir " [\-p] " path " ..."
Create directories.
With
.B \-p
missing parents are created and existing directories are not an error.
.TP
.BI stat " path " ..."
Show type, size, modification time and permission bits.
.TP
.BI tree " [path] [\-L " depth "]"
Draw the directory hierarchy, limited to
.I depth
levels if given.
.TP
.B help
List the available commands.
.TP
.B clear
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B exit
Leave the shell.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Include hidden entries (names starting with a dot).
.TP
.B \-l
Long listing: permissions, size, modification time.
.TP
.B \-r
Recurse into directories.
.TP
.B \-f
Overwrite existing destinations, and ignore paths that do not exist when
removing.
.TP
.B \-p
Copy permission bits.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the command list.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List the current directory, hidden entries included:
.B fman ls -a
.TP
Copy a tree and keep permission bits:
.B fman cp -rp src backup/
.TP
Show two levels of the hierarchy below the home directory:
.B fman tree ~ -L 2
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on failure, which includes a cancelled operation.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ls (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR mv (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR tree (1)
.SH AUTHOR
mrg <mrg@localhost>
